Question: How does an embedding model learn semantic meaning?

Strong Answer:

An embedding model is trained on massive amounts of text. It starts with random vectors and gradually adjusts them during training by 
learning from the contexts in which words appear. Words that occur in similar contexts, such as "dog" and "puppy," end up with similar embeddings.
This allows the model to capture general semantic relationships without being explicitly programmed with definitions.

But is one embedding enough?

Suppose we have these two sentences. [Self-Attention looks at every word.]
Sentence 1: I deposited money in the bank. Bank = Financial Institution
Sentence 2: The fisherman sat on the bank of the river. Bank = River Side

How can the model know which meaning is correct?

Embeddings alone cannot fully solve this.

This is why Self-Attention exists
Self-attention asks: "Which surrounding words help me understand this word?"

Embedding alone can't reliably distinguish these meanings. Self-attention can.

At this point, the model only knows that "bank" is a word with multiple possible meanings.

It does not know whether it means:

Financial institution
River bank

